==English==

===Etymology 1===
Noun from {{inh|en|enm|wund}}, from {{inh|en|ang|wund}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wundō}}. Verb from {{inh|en|enm|wunden}}, from {{inh|en|ang|wundian}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wundōną}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/wyːnd/|a=MLE}}
* {{enPR|wo͞ond|a=UK}}, {{IPA|en|/wuːnd/}}
* {{enPR|wo͞ond|a=US}}, {{IPA|en|/wund/}}
* {{enPR|wound|a=obsolete}}, {{IPA|en|/waʊnd/}}<ref>{{cite-journal|year=1899|title=From Franklin to Lowell|last=Grandgent|first=C. H.|editor=James W. Bright|page=238|journal=Proceedings of the Modern Language Association|volume=14|issue=2|publisher={{w|Modern Language Association of America}}|doi=10.1632/456572|url= https://www.google.co.nz/books/edition/Publications_of_the_Modern_Language_Asso/0OENAAAAIAAJ}}</ref><ref>{{R:Krapp English Language in America|II|150}}</ref>
* {{audio|en|en-us-wound-injury.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːnd|s=1}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|injury}}An [[injury]], such as a [[cut]], [[stab]], or [[tear]], to a (usually external) part of the body.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1 September 2013|author=Phil McNulty|titleurl=http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/23830980|title=Liverpool 1-0 Man Utd|journal=BBC Sport
|passage=The visitors were without {{w|Wayne Rooney}} after he suffered a head '''wound''' in training, which also keeps him out of England's World Cup qualifiers against Moldova and Ukraine.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|3|3|text=Showers of blood / Rained from the '''wounds''' of slaughtered Englishmen.}}
#* {{RQ:Stevenson Treasure Island|text=I went below, and did what I could for my '''wound'''; it pained me a good deal, and still bled freely; but it was neither deep nor dangerous, nor did it greatly gall me when I used my arm.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A [[hurt]] to a person's [[feeling]]s, [[reputation]], [[prospect]]s, etc.
#: {{ux|en|It took a long time to get over the '''wound''' of that insult.}}
# {{lb|en|criminal|_|legal}} An injury to a person by which the [[skin]] is [[divide]]d or its [[continuity]] [[broken]].

