====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To hurt or injure (someone) by cutting, piercing, or tearing the skin.
#: {{syn|en|damage|harm|Thesaurus:harm|Thesaurus:hurt}}
#: {{ux|en|The police officer '''wounded''' the suspect during the fight that ensued.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To hurt (a person's feelings).
#: {{syn|en|affront|hurt|Thesaurus:offend}}
#: {{ux|en|The actor's pride was '''wounded''' when the leading role went to his rival.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=08 Dec 84|volume=12|number=21|journal=Gay Community News|page=12|author=Michael Bronski;Andrea Loewenstein|title=Family & Friends: Writers Talk Community|text=I find neglect or rejection from my own community much harder to take and more '''wounding''' than the same thing or worse from the outside world.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In older forms of English, when the pronoun ''[[thou]]'' was in active use, and verbs used ''[[-est]]'' for distinct second-person singular indicative forms, the verb ''wound'' had the form ''[[woundest]]'', and had ''[[woundedst]]'' for its past tense.
* Similarly, when the ending ''[[-eth]]'' was in active use for third-person singular present indicative forms, the form ''[[woundeth]]'' was used.

